<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2003" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">How tight is the bound?</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1872" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We just saw how the formula <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is an upper bound for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==</Equation>, the number of positive rationals listed by the end of stage <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  We'll conclude this section by exploring how good of a bound this is.  In Section 2.3, we saw how to use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:plot" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">plot</Font></Hyperlink> command to graph points.  Let's use that technique to graph the upper bound up to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1873" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To graph points, we need two lists for the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> values to be graphed.  The <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> values will be the values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  We'll create the list by using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:seq" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">seq</Font></Hyperlink> command.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1874" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">xValues := [seq(n,n=2..100)]:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1875" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">For the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-values, we use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:seq" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">seq</Font></Hyperlink> command with the formula for the upper bound: <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1876" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">boundValues := [seq(n*(n-1)/2,n=2..100)]:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1877" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Now we can plot the bound using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:plot" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">plot</Font></Hyperlink> command and the options described in Section 2.3.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1878" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">plot(xValues,boundValues,style=point,symbol=solidcircle,symbolsize=15,view=[0..100,0..5000]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="238" type="two-dimensional" width="238" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="false"></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1896" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To find the actual values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==</Equation>, we need the size of the list returned by <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">ListRationals</Font> applied to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  In other words, we apply <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nops" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">nops</Font></Hyperlink> to the result of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">ListRationals(n)</Font>.  Again, we use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:seq" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">seq</Font></Hyperlink> to form the list of these counts.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1897" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">actualValues := [seq(nops(ListRationals(n)),n=2..100)]:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1898" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Again, we plot.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1899" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">plot(xValues,actualValues,style=point,symbol=solidcircle,symbolsize=15,view=[0..100,0..5000]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="238" type="two-dimensional" width="238" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="false"></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1904" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Since we used the same <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">view</Font> for both graphs, you can immediately see that the value of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==</Equation> is much smaller than the upper bound.  We can make the comparison easier by overlaying the graphs.  We do this as follows.  First, we assign the graphs to names.  (We'll also change the color of the graph of the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==</Equation> data to blue.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1905" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">boundPlot:= plot(xValues,boundValues,style=point,symbol=solidcircle,symbolsize=15,view=[0..100,0..5000],legend=&quot;bound&quot;):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1906" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">actualPlot := plot(xValues,actualValues,style=point,symbol=solidcircle,symbolsize=15,view=[0..100,0..5000],color=blue,legend=&quot;actual&quot;):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1907" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Then we have Maple draw the two plots together using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:plots,display" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">display</Font></Hyperlink> command in the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:plots" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">plots</Font></Hyperlink> package.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1908" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">plots[display](boundPlot,actualPlot);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="234" type="two-dimensional" width="234" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="true"></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1926" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">You will explore <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiRkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRIm5GJ0YvRjIvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYnRj1GPQ==</Equation> and the upper bound <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> further in the exercises.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.6 Matrices</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1382" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple provides extensive support for calculating with matrices.  We'll begin this section by describing a variety of ways to construct matrices in Maple.  Then we'll consider matrix arithmetic and operations on zero-one matrices.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1420" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Constructing Matrices</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1421" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Matrices are constructed using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Matrix" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Matrix</Font></Hyperlink> command.  This command can be used in several different forms and with a large variety of options, only some of which we will discuss here.  For complete information, refer to the Maple help page.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2127" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Specifying entries by listing the rows</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1422" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The simplest way to construct a matrix in Maple is by representing each row as a list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1423" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m1 := Matrix([[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK09sUjBW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1425" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In the above example, we passed one argument to the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Matrix" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Matrix</Font></Hyperlink> command: a list of lists where the inner lists are the rows of the matrix.  This list of lists is referred to as the matrix initializer.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1426" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You can also explicitly set the size of the matrix by giving the number of rows and columns as arguments to the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Matrix" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Matrix</Font></Hyperlink> command.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1428" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m2 := Matrix(2,3,[[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK18obyg0Vg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1430" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that if the specified dimension is smaller than what is indicated by the initializer list, an error is generated.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1431" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m3 := Matrix(2,2,[[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="HyperlinkError" layout="HyperlinkError"><Hyperlink linktarget="http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20(in%20Matrix)%20initializer%20defines%20more%20columns%20(3)%20than%20column%20dimension%20parameter%20specifies%20(2)" hyperlink="true"><Font style="HyperlinkError">Error, (in Matrix) initializer defines more columns (3) than column dimension parameter specifies (2)</Font></Hyperlink></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1433" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">But if the specified dimension is larger, Maple will create the matrix of the desired size and fill the rest of the entries with 0s.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1434" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m4 := Matrix(3,4,[[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK29uNGhW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1436" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Also, if only one dimension is given, Maple assumes that a square matrix of that size is desired.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1437" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m5 := Matrix(4,[[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKzdGPzNW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1439" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You can have Maple pad the matrix with a different value by using the optional <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">fill=value</Font> argument.  Below, we create a square matrix of dimension 3 whose entries are all 5.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1440" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m6 := Matrix(3,fill=5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK3dwYmhW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1442" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The initializer does not have to be a list of lists as in the previous examples.  For instance, you can use another matrix, as in the example below where we expand the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m6</Font> matrix.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1443" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7 := Matrix(3,4,m6,fill=2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKy8oKVwwVg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2129" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Modifying entries</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2130" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Once a matrix has been created, its entries can be altered by assigning the new value to the specified location, with the square bracket selection notation used to indicate the desired location.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2131" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, to change the lower left entry of matrix <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEjbTZGJy8lJ2l0YWxpY0dRJXRydWVGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1EnaXRhbGljRicvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEjbTZGJy8lJ2l0YWxpY0dRJXRydWVGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1EnaXRhbGljRicvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> to 6, you would enter the following command.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L2132" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m6[3,1] := 6;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiIn</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2133" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that Maple reports the assignment.  To see that it has happened, we have to explicitly command Maple to display the entire matrix.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L2134" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m6;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK3dwYmhW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2136" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Copying matrices</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1445" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Using a matrix as the initializer for <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Matrix" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Matrix</Font></Hyperlink> is commonly used to make a copy of a matrix.  Consider the following sequence of commands.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1446" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7copy := m7;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKy8oKVwwVg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1447" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7[1,2] := 11;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiM2</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1448" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKy8oKVwwVg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1449" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7copy;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKy8oKVwwVg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1779" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Observe that the modification we made to the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7</Font> matrix was also made in the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7copy</Font> matrix.  This is because the assignment <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7copy := m7;</Font> did not create a new copy of the matrix to store in <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7copy</Font>.  Instead, that assignment made both names refer to the same matrix.  The assignment <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7[1,2] := 11;</Font> modified the row 1, column 2 entry of the unique matrix that both names refer to.  In computer science, this is called a &quot;reference type,&quot; meaning that the name does not store the object, it stores a reference to the object.  Assigning one name to another makes a copy of the reference, but both references refer to the same underlying object.  Both matrices and tables are reference types in Maple.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1451" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To make a true copy of a matrix, you use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Matrix" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Matrix</Font></Hyperlink> command with the matrix you want to copy as the initializer.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1452" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7realcopy := Matrix(m7);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKztNWzBW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1453" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7[2,4] := 23;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiNC</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1454" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m7, m7copy, m7realcopy;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">NiUtSSdNYXRyaXhHNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHNiI2Iy9JJCVpZEdGKCIrLygpXDBWRiMtRiQ2Iy9GKyIrO01bMFY=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1456" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">While the modification of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7</Font> altered both <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7</Font> and <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7copy</Font>, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m7realcopy</Font> was unchanged.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2138" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Other ways to initialize matrices</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1427" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Another common way to create a matrix is by specifying the values with a single list rather than a list of lists.  In this case, you must provide the dimension of the matrix, whereas it is optional if the initializer is a list of lists.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1457" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m8 := Matrix(2,3,[1,2,3,4,5,6]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK2M7WzBW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1459" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You can also specify a matrix with a table.  Note the use of parentheses around the table indices.  Also, in this case, the row and column dimensions are required.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1460" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m9table := table([(1,2)=5,(1,3)=6,(2,1)=-2]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">PTYiSSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEdFXFtsJDYkIiIiIiIjIiImNiRGKCIiJCIiJzYkRilGKCEiIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1461" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m9 := Matrix(2,3,m9table);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK2MlemFJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1465" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Finally, you can initialize the matrix with a procedure.  The procedure must accept two integers as arguments.  The entries of the matrix are obtained by evaluating the procedure at the row and column number.  Below we provide an example using a functional operator to make the entries in the matrix equal to the sum of the row and column numbers and then construct the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> matrix from it.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1466" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m10F := (i,j) -&gt; i + j;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Zio2JEkiaUc2IkkiakdGJUYlNiRJKW9wZXJhdG9yR0YlSSZhcnJvd0dGJUYlLCY5JCIiIjklRixGJUYlRiU=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1467" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m10 := Matrix(4,m10F);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK1tlczBW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1469" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Matrix Arithmetic</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1470" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The textbook defines addition and multiplication of matrices.  Maple implements these operations on matrices in a fairly intuitive way.  To add two matrices, you use the + operator, as you would expect.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1471" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m11 := Matrix([[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKzdiJmVJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1472" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m12 := Matrix([[-2,3,-1],[1,5,2]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKyFbUWVJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1473" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m11 + m12;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK1NdQjFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1545" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Maple's syntax for multiplying a matrix by a scalar is also intuitive. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1546" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">3*m12;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK3cqb1lPJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1548" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This produces the matrix whose entries are three times the entries of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m12</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1475" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Matrix multiplication is computed with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:dot" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">.</Font></Hyperlink> (dot) operator instead of an asterisk.  This is to emphasize that matrix multiplication is not commutative.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1476" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m13 := Matrix([[3,6,11,1],[-2,5,2,0],[4,8,9,-3]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKy9IPzFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1477" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m14 := Matrix([[2,5],[1,-2],[3,7],[-1,0]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK2szPzFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1478" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m13 . m14;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKztjOzFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1480" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Transposes, Powers, and Equality of Matrices</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1481" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Positive powers of matrices in Maple work exactly as you would expect, with the caret symbol, provided, of course, that the matrix is square.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1482" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m15 := Matrix([[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKyFbNGtJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1483" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m15^5;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKzNnYTFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1485" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The transpose of a matrix is most easily computed with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra,Transpose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">^+</Font></Hyperlink> operator, as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1486" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m14^+;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK0dgYTFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1488" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Alternately, you can use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra,Transpose" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Transpose</Font></Hyperlink> command, but this requires the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">LinearAlgebra</Font></Hyperlink> package.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1489" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">LinearAlgebra[Transpose](m14);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK2NTejFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1491" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Finally, we need to make a note about equality of matrices.  For matrices, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">=</Font> does not test that two matrices are equal in the usual sense.  For example, observe that the commands below do not yield the expected result.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1492" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m16 := Matrix([[1,2],[3,4]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKzdOeDFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1493" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">m17 := Matrix([[1,2],[3,4]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK0sxeDFW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1494" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(m16=m17);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1496" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In fact, what <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">=</Font> tests for in the case of matrices is whether or not two names are referring to the same matrix object.  This is another case in which being a reference type makes things a bit different.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1497" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To test for equality of matrices in the mathematical sense, it is necessary to use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra,Equal" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Equal</Font></Hyperlink> command in the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">LinearAlgebra</Font></Hyperlink> package.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1498" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">LinearAlgebra[Equal](m16,m17);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1505" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Zero-One Matrices</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1506" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">With Maple, we can create and manipulate zero-one matrices as well.  In particular, we'll consider how to compute the meet, join, and Boolean product of zero-one matrices.  Unlike matrix addition and multiplication, Maple does not have built-in commands for these computations, so we'll need to create our own procedures.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2140" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Introducing Bits package commands</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1552" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Calculation of meet, join, and the Boolean product require the use of the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> bit operations.  In the previous chapter we created our own <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">AND</Font> procedure as a way to explore some fundamental programming constructs.  In this section, we'll instead make use of Maple's <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Bits</Font></Hyperlink> package.  This package provides several commands related to performing operations on bits and bit strings.  We'll only make use of two commands: <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">And</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Or" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Or</Font></Hyperlink>.  First we load the package.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1554" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">with(Bits):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1556" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">And</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Or" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Or</Font></Hyperlink> commands take two arguments and return the bit-wise <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> or <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, respectively.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1557" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">And(1,1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1558" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">And(0,1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIh</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1559" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Or(0,1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1560" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Or(0,0);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIh</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1566" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">(Note: the arguments to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">And</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Or" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Or</Font></Hyperlink> are not restricted to 0 and 1.  They can be any integers and Maple will perform the operation on the bit strings that represent the integers.  We will not explore that further here as it is not necessary for the task at hand.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2142" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">A type for zero-one matrices</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1562" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">To create a zero-one matrix, we can use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Matrix" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Matrix</Font></Hyperlink> command as usual.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1563" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">zo1 := Matrix([[1,0,1],[1,1,0],[0,1,0]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK2c6IWVJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1565" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As has been mentioned before, it is always a good idea to do type-checking in procedures.  This is particularly important for the next procedures that we will write.  Since we'll be using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">And</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Or" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Or</Font></Hyperlink> commands from <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Bits</Font></Hyperlink>, and these commands will <Font italic="true">not</Font> produce errors if their inputs are integers other than 0 and 1, it is important for our procedures to make sure that their input are zero-one matrices.  Otherwise, the procedures may execute on 'bad' inputs and produce nonsense output.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1567" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">In Maple, a matrix has type <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:type,Matrix" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Matrix</Font></Hyperlink>, but in this case, we'll want to be more specific.  We'll use the following variant type: <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">'Matrix'(type)</Font>.  This allows us to specify the type of the entries that are allowed in the matrix.  For example, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">'Matrix'(float)</Font> indicates that the entries in the matrix must all be floating point numbers.  Since we want to check for zero-one matrices, we'll use <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">{0,1}</Font> as the type of entry allowed in the matrix.  Remember that braces in a <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:type,structure" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">structured type</Font></Hyperlink> indicate than any of the options inside the braces are acceptable.  (Note: the single right quotes delay evaluation so that Maple doesn't consider the word <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">Matrix</Font> to be the matrix construction command.)  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1568" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Observe that <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">zo1</Font> is a zero-one matrix, but <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">m17</Font> is not.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1569" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">type(zo1,'Matrix'({0,1}));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1570" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">type(m17,'Matrix'({0,1}));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2144" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Implementing Join</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1572" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">With those preliminaries completed, we write the join procedure.  This procedure will accept two arguments, both of which must be zero-one matrices.  The procedure will also need to check the sizes of the matrices.  To do this, we use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra,Dimension" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">RowDimension</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra,Dimension" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">ColumnDimension</Font></Hyperlink> commands from the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">LinearAlgebra</Font></Hyperlink> package.  These commands accept only one argument, the matrix, and return the number of rows or columns, respectively.  If the dimensions do not match, an error will be generated.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1573" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">After confirming that the two matrices are the same size, the procedure will create a new matrix that is the same size as the input matrices.  By omitting any initialization information, Maple will automatically fill this matrix with 0s.  The procedure will then consider each position in the matrix using two nested for loops to loop through the rows and the columns.  For each position, it computes the bitwise <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> of the entries in the original matrices and sets the corresponding entry in the result matrix <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">R</Font>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1574" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here is the procedure.  Note that we make use of the <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">uses</Font> statement.  This indicates what packages the procedure relies on and ensures that those packages have been loaded, so we can use the short forms of commands.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1575" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">BoolJoin := proc(A::'Matrix'({0,1}),B::'Matrix'({0,1}))
  local numrows, numcols, R, r, c;
  uses LinearAlgebra, Bits;
  numrows := RowDimension(A);
  numcols := ColumnDimension(A);
  if numrows &lt;&gt; RowDimension(B) or
     numcols &lt;&gt; ColumnDimension(B) then
    error &quot;Input matrices must be of the same size.&quot;;
  end if;
  R := Matrix(numrows,numcols);
  for r from 1 to numrows do
    for c from 1 to numcols do
      R[r,c] := Or(A[r,c],B[r,c]);
    end do;
  end do;
  return R;
end proc: </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1577" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Below we apply this procedure to two example matrices.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L1578" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">zo1;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK2c6IWVJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1579" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">zo2 := Matrix([[1,0,0],[1,1,1],[0,0,0]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK2MjNHBJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1580" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">BoolJoin(zo1,zo2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK09KIXBJJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1582" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We leave the creation of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BoolMeet</Font> to the reader.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L2146" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Implementing Boolean product</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1553" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We conclude by implementing the Boolean product.  Recall two key points from Definition 9 in Section 2.6.  First, the size of the product of an <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> matrix and an <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and the product is undefined if the number of columns of the first matrix does not match the number of rows in the second.  Second, the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> entry of the product is given by the formula </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1583" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1584" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Our Boolean product procedure, <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BProduct</Font>, needs to begin by using <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra,Dimension" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">RowDimension</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:LinearAlgebra,ColumnDimension" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">ColumnDimension</Font></Hyperlink> to find the values for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and to raise an error if the number of columns of the first matrix does not match the number of rows of the second.  Like <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BoolJoin</Font>, we create the result matrix <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">C</Font> to be of the appropriate size and allow Maple to fill all the entries with 0.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1585" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The main work of the procedure is to loop over all the entries of the result matrix and calculate the appropriate value.  We use two nested for loops with index variables <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">i</Font> and <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">j</Font> representing the rows and columns of the result matrix.  Inside these for loops, we need to implement the formula for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRI2lqRidGMkY1RjJGNS8lL3N1YnNjcmlwdHNoaWZ0R1EiMEYnL0Y2USdub3JtYWxGJw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiY0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRI2lqRidGMkY1RjJGNS8lL3N1YnNjcmlwdHNoaWZ0R1EiMEYnL0Y2USdub3JtYWxGJw==</Equation>.  It will be helpful to consider a specific example:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1586" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1587" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">And</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Or" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Or</Font></Hyperlink> can accept only two arguments, so we cannot use a single <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Or" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">Or</Font></Hyperlink> applied to a sequence of <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font executable="false" style="Maple Input" underline="true">And</Font></Hyperlink>s, as you might hope.  Instead, we'll approach this in the following way.  First, compute <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, the first <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, and store the result as <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">c</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1588" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">c := And(1,0);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiIh</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1590" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Then, update c to be the result of applying <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> to it and the result of the next <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> term.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1591" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">c := Or(c,And(0,0));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiIh</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1593" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">And then repeat with each successive <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1594" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">c := Or(c,And(0,1));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiIh</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1595" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">c := Or(c,And(1,1));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1596" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">c := Or(c,And(0,1));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1598" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In terms of the generic formula, we initialize <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Then we begin a loop with index, say <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, from <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiMkYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiMkYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation> through <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEia0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  At each step in the loop, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1600" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here is the implementation of <Font executable="false" style="Maple Input">BProduct</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1601" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">BoolProduct := proc(A::'Matrix'({0,1}),B::'Matrix'({0,1}))
  local m, k, n, C, i, j, c, p;
  uses LinearAlgebra, Bits;
  m := RowDimension(A);
  k := ColumnDimension(A);
  if k &lt;&gt; RowDimension(B) then
    error &quot;Dimension mismatch.&quot;;
  end if;
  n := ColumnDimension(B);
  C := Matrix(m,n);
  for i from 1 to m do
    for j from 1 to n do
      c := And(A[i,1],B[1,j]);
      for p from 2 to k do
        c := Or(c,And(A[i,p],B[p,j]));
      end do;
      C[i,j] := c;
    end do;
  end do;
  return C;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1603" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We test this procedure on the matrices from Example 8 in the textbook.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L1604" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Ex8A := Matrix([[1,0],[0,1],[1,0]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK3deKm9JJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L1605" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Ex8B := Matrix([[1,1,0],[0,1,1]]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKzNfKW9JJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L1606" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">BoolProduct(Ex8A,Ex8B);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciK1NdKW9JJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
